The '''Kannondaira Site''' contains three groups of approximately 53 tumuli, including one [[Zenpokoenfun|keyhole-shaped tomb]] ({{nihongo|''zenpō-kōen-fun''|前方後円墳|lt=Zenpokoenfun|1=Zenpokoenfun|comment=doing this because I need to differentiate it in piped links too with my new interwiki tool as not the ambiguous phrase keyhole shaped kofun|1=Zenpokoenfun|1=Zenpokoenfun}}) dating from the 3rd century. Most of the tombs are {{ill|Round Kofun|lt=dome-shaped|ja|円墳|1=Round Kofun|1=Round Kofun|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Round Kofun|13=User:Immanuelle/Round Kofun|qq=}} ({{nihongo|''empun''|円墳|1=Round Kofun|1=Round Kofun}}), although one is {{ill|Scallop Kofun|lt=scallop-shaped|ja|帆立貝形古墳|1=Scallop Kofun|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Scallop Kofun|13=User:Immanuelle/Scallop Kofun|qq=}} ({{nihongo|''hotategai-gata kofun''|帆立貝形古墳|1=Scallop Kofun}}) and some are {{ill|Square Kofun|lt=square-shaped|ja|方墳|1=Square Kofun|1=Square Kofun|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Square Kofun|13=User:Immanuelle/Square Kofun|qq=}} ({{nihongo|''hōfun''|方墳|1=Square Kofun|1=Square Kofun}}), indicating that the site is from a transitional period between the [[Yayoi period]] and the Kofun period. ''[[Haniwa]]'' and ''[[fukiishi]]'' have been recovered from the area. The keyhole-shaped tomb is designated No.4 in the group, and has a total length of {{Convert|33.6|m|sp=us}}, with a dome-portion measuring {{Convert|19.0|by|23.0|m|sp=us}}, and a trapezoidal portion with length of {{Convert|11.6|m|sp=us}} and width of {{Convert|7.0|m|sp=us}}, narrowing to {{Convert|5.0|m|sp=us}} at the "neck" where it connects with the dome. No [[grave goods]] were found in this tomb.<ref name="Shiseki"/>
The '''Tenjindō Site''' also consists of three groups of tumuli, of which 188 have thus far been catalogued, making it one of the largest sites in Niigata Prefecture. Almost all of the tumuli are small and dome-shaped, with diameters between {{Convert|5|and|25|m|sp=us|spell=in}}. The site has been repeatedly [[archaeological excavation|excavated]] by [[Tokyo University]]. [[Grave goods]] recovered include [[Sue pottery]], [[Shinju-kyo|bronze mirrors]], straight iron swords, parts of armor, horse fittings and items of jewellery. From these grave goods, it is estimated that these tumuli were constructed from the late 5th century to the late 6th century.<ref name="Shiseki"/>
The two sites flank a large moated late [[Yayoi period]] to early Kofun-period settlement, the [[Hida Sites|Hida Site]], which has a separate National Historic Site designation. The Kannondaira cluster is to the north, and the Tenjindō cluster is to the south, and these areas may have been the necropolis for this settlement. As the area was mostly covered in forest, the state of preservation was relatively good, although even in 1976 it had been reported that a number of the known ''kofun'' has been destroyed due to urban encroachment, road construction, landslides and robbery. After the designation as a National Historic Site, the area has been cleared and opened to the public as an [[archaeological park]], with a small museum to display some of the artifacts found.<ref name="Shiseki"/>
==See also== *[[List of Historic Sites of Japan (Niigata)]]
